  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081888/assets/41e7b3f6588f7f4dd55096fb95bbe517a053c11f80c43aab171d80829864d2c3.png)\n\nThe total area $A$ of the region between the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}$ is given by the integral expression below. What is the value of $a?$\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{a}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{a}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "To determine the missing limit of integration, we need to find the $x$-intercept of the curve $y=3x-2{x}^{2}.$ So, we set $y=0$ and solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x(3-2x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{3}{2}\\in [1,4],$ we have two regions:\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=1$ to $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n- The area between the curve and the $x$-axis from $x=\\frac{3}{2}$ to $x=\\sqrt{10}.$\n\nSo, $a=\\frac{3}{2}$ and the final expression will be\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{3/2}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x-{\\int }_{3/2}^{\\sqrt{10}}(3x-2{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$",
